{
  "id": "PPA-15",
  "title": "Stomata Guard Cell Osmoregulation & Aperture Engine",
  "subtitle": "Blue light hyperpolarization vs ABA-triggered depolarization driving turgor aperture dynamics",
  "caption": "Biophysical and biochemical control of stomatal pore aperture in paired guard cells. In response to morning blue light, photoreceptors PHOT1/2 activate BLUS1, stimulating the plasma membrane H+-ATPases (AHA1/2). Resulting hyperpolarization (-150 mV) drives massive K+ influx through KAT1/2 channels, balanced by PEPC-derived malate and chloride uptake, drawing in water via PIP2;1 aquaporins and swelling guard cells to open the pore. Under drought, stress-induced ABA triggers PYR/RCAR-mediated activation of SnRK2.6/OST1 kinase, which phosphorylates SLAC1/SLAH3 anion channels and stimulates RBOHD ROS generation. Membrane depolarization (-40 mV) opens GORK potassium release channels, causing turgor collapse and pore closure.",
  "species_anchor": "arabidopsis_thaliana",
